#!/usr/bin/env python3
# Copyright 2026 Theorem Labs, PBC
# SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
"""Check the owners of this repository's files and the credentials of its workflows:

    python3 -I .github/scripts/workflow-credentials [<checkout>]

<checkout> is the top of a checkout, the current directory by default. The script reads .github/CODEOWNERS and each
file .github/workflows/*.yml and *.yaml of it as data, and runs no code of them. It prints each problem as a line
::error::<problem> and exits 1. Else it prints one line that says what it read, and exits 0. With no workflow file to
read it fails: a check that read nothing has not passed. tests/test_token_jobs.py checks the other side of a credential:
that no job that holds one, or whose outputs such a job reads, loads code from src/.

(c), the owners (R10 of dispatch://INFRA-5359/spec). .github/CODEOWNERS names a person only for itself and the agent
instruction files, the patterns of ALLOWED: each line of it is one of them, with @theorem-labs/engineering and
@theorem-labs/research as its owners and no one else, and each of them has a line. The agent instruction files are
AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, SKILL.md and skills/ at any depth, .claude/, CLAUDE*.md and AGENTS*.md, and the change review's
own rules, review.md, review-change.md and review-issue.md: each of them instructs an agent, and a change that weakens
the review's rules must not merge through that same review. The last line that matches a file decides its owners, so
a line with no owner after one of them would take the owner back from each file that both match: the script refuses
each line with no owner. Every other file, the workflows and this script among them, merges through the change
review, whose check review main's ruleset requires (R14, review.yml), and a job that can get a credential runs main's
copy of its workflow in a main-only environment ((m), below).

A job holds a credential, for the analyses below, when it, or the env and the permissions of its workflow that it
takes, reads a secret, or when it has a secrets: key, an environment, a permission that writes (id-token: write is one)
or a permissions text other than read-all. A job with no permissions, in a workflow with none that starts on an event
of its own (not only on workflow_call), holds the token's default permissions, which the settings of the repository
decide. A read of a secret is a reference to the secrets context in an expression: in a ${{ }} of any key or value of
the job (a step's with: and env:, a call's with:), or in an if:, which GitHub reads as an expression with or without
${{ }}. An expression ends at the first }} outside a string in single quotes, as GitHub reads it, or at the end of the
value. GitHub reads a context's name in any case, so Secrets and SECRETS count, in any form (secrets.X, secrets['X'],
toJSON(secrets), format('{0}', secrets.X)); a name after a dot (inputs.secrets) is a property, not the context. The text
of a run: outside ${{ }} is the shell's, and is not read. A job that calls a workflow (uses:) runs that workflow's jobs
in its own run: it holds what they hold, at every level of calls (a called job's environment gives that job the
environment's secrets). A job that calls a workflow of another repository holds what this script cannot read, and
counts as holding a credential. A job that can save in main's Actions cache holds one too: review.yml restores entries
of that cache for a review, and trusts that only its own session job saved them. A job of a workflow that starts on an
event other than pull_request can, unless its cache-mode (its own, else its workflow's) is none or read.

The reading. GitHub reads a workflow as YAML 1.2, and PyYAML reads YAML 1.1, so where the two could differ the script
refuses the file: an explicit tag (!!set, !!str, !custom), which can make PyYAML drop or change a value; the merge key
<<; a key that is no plain scalar; and a key repeated in any case, of which PyYAML keeps only the last. Each other key
is the text it is written in: PyYAML would read on, yes and true all as the boolean true, so one job could hide
another.

(r), for each workflow. No workflow starts on pull_request_target. A workflow that starts on pull_request reads no
secret and gives its permissions, and its jobs have no environment, no secrets: key and no id-token. Its
workflow-level permissions write nothing: they reach every job. A job of it that writes has the if
github.event_name == 'push', so that no run on pull_request holds the permission, and it writes security-events alone
(CodeQL's push jobs, which record main's results). Any other write needs a workflow that does not start on
pull_request. A job that calls a workflow of this repository runs that file's jobs in its own run, with its own
permissions at most: they are held to the same, and run only on push when the calling job does.

(m), main's copy alone, for each job that can get a credential: id-token: write (write-all is one), which Google's
federation trades for a cloud identity, or a read of the secrets context, by the job or by the env of its workflow
(the forms above). Such a job declares an environment whose name is one of MAIN_ONLY_ENVIRONMENTS, each of which has
main as its one deployment branch (theorem-labs/agent-infra's github/ program declares them; GitHub's answer for each
is in tests/fixtures/review-check/environments/). It does not start on an event that runs a pull request's copy of
its workflow (PULL_REQUEST_EVENTS), and checks out nothing but its run's own commit: no actions/checkout with a ref
or a repository, and no gh pr checkout, refs/pull/ or git fetch, checkout, switch, worktree, pull or reset in a run.
That is a tripwire, fail-closed for what a run's text shows; what a script that a step runs does is check 4 of the
change review's (review-change.md). A job of a called workflow takes its own permissions, else its workflow's, else
the calling job's, and is held to the same with the calling workflow's events. A job that calls a workflow declares
no environment (GitHub refuses one there), so it passes no secrets, and calls no workflow of another repository with
id-token: write.
The GITHUB_TOKEN's write scopes are no credential here: any writer can ask for them in a branch's own workflow, and
main's ruleset keeps them from main (dispatch://INFRA-5359/spec, R10). On success the script lists each such job with
its environment.

The analyses. No job that holds a credential runs a CodeQL analysis that can run code of the packages it reads. Only
GitHub Actions, JavaScript, TypeScript and Ruby (SOURCE_ONLY) in any build mode, C and C++ (BUILDLESS) in build mode
none, and Python in a job that runs PYTHON_GUARD before init, with no PYTHONPATH and no write of $GITHUB_ENV, pass:
Rust's extractor runs cargo check, build scripts and proc macros in any build mode; C#'s runs dotnet restore;
autobuild and manual run the project's build; Python's extractor imports from the top of the checkout; and a
language that the check does not know or cannot read fails. Such code comes from files that need no code owner (every
file but CODEOWNERS and the agent instruction files, (c)), and would run with the credential. The script reads the
languages and build-mode of each step github/codeql-action/init, at every level of calls, with ${{ inputs.<name> }}
taken from the calling job's with:, resolved in its caller first, and the languages lowercase, split at commas and
blanks; an expression it cannot resolve, and a call of a workflow it cannot read, fail.

tests.yaml's job suite runs this on the commit of each run: for a pull request, GitHub's merge of it. So a pull request
that names an owner in CODEOWNERS for another file, takes the owner from an agent instruction file, breaks (r) or (m),
or analyses such a language in a job with a credential fails the required check tests. A change to this script or to
tests.yaml merges through the change review, as any other file does; a change to CODEOWNERS needs its owners too.
tests/test_trigger_move.py (PullRequestWorkflows) and tests/test_check_checks.py (WorkflowCredentials, and the
functions after it of (c) and (m)) test it.
"""
from __future__ import annotations

import functools
import pathlib
import re
import sys

import yaml

WORKFLOWS = ".github/workflows"
CODEOWNERS = ".github/CODEOWNERS"
TEAMS = ("@theorem-labs/engineering", "@theorem-labs/research")
# (c): the patterns that .github/CODEOWNERS gives to TEAMS, and its only lines: CODEOWNERS itself and the agent
# instruction files (the top of this file).
ALLOWED = ("/.github/CODEOWNERS", "**/AGENTS.md", "**/CLAUDE.md", "**/SKILL.md", "skills/", ".claude/", "CLAUDE*.md",
           "AGENTS*.md", "/review.md", "/review-change.md", "/review-issue.md")
# PyYAML's C parser when it is built, else its Python one.
LOADER = getattr(yaml, "CSafeLoader", yaml.SafeLoader)
# A reference to the secrets context in the text of an expression: the name in any case, not after a dot, a letter, a
# digit, _ or -, and not before one of them (secrets.X, Secrets['X'], toJSON(SECRETS)).
SECRET = re.compile(r"(?<![\w.\-])secrets(?![\w\-])", re.IGNORECASE)
# Where an expression starts in a key or a value; it ends at the first }} outside a string (expression_texts).
EXPRESSION_START = "${{"
# A job that calls a workflow of this repository.
LOCAL_WORKFLOW = re.compile(r"\./(\.github/workflows/[^/@]+\.ya?ml)")
# The if of a job that runs only on push. In a workflow that starts on pull_request, a job with a write permission has
# exactly this if, and the permission is security-events: write alone ((r) above).
PUSH_ONLY = "github.event_name == 'push'"
PUSH_WRITES = "a push job may write only security-events"
# (m): the GitHub Environments whose one deployment branch is main, which theorem-labs/agent-infra's github/ program
# declares and Theorem's federation admits only from main. A job that can get a credential declares one of them.
MAIN_ONLY_ENVIRONMENTS = ("comment", "enroll-review", "required-checks", "review-screen")
# The events on which a job runs the pull request's copy of its workflow, or holds the base's credentials beside the
# pull request's code: no job that can get a credential starts on them.
PULL_REQUEST_EVENTS = ("pull_request", "pull_request_target", "pull_request_review", "pull_request_review_comment")
# A run's text that checks out or fetches other code than its run's own commit: gh pr checkout, refs/pull/, and any git
# fetch, checkout, switch, worktree, pull or reset. Fail-closed: a job that can get a credential needs none of them.
PULL_REQUEST_CODE = re.compile(r"\bgh\s+pr\s+checkout\b|\brefs/pull/|\bgit\s+(fetch|checkout|switch|worktree|pull|reset)\b",
                               re.IGNORECASE)


class Unreadable(Exception):
    """A workflow file that cannot be read, that is no YAML mapping, or that the script refuses to read (the top of this
    file)."""


class Refused(yaml.YAMLError):
    """YAML that GitHub (YAML 1.2) and PyYAML (YAML 1.1) could read differently."""


def construct_mapping(loader, node):
    """A mapping keyed on the text of each key as written. A key that is no plain scalar, the merge key <<, and a key
    repeated in any case are Refused."""
    if not isinstance(node, yaml.MappingNode):
        raise Refused(f"line {node.start_mark.line + 1}: a mapping that is no mapping")
    mapping, seen = {}, {}
    for key_node, value_node in node.value:
        line = key_node.start_mark.line + 1
        # The C parser gives a plain scalar the style '', the Python one None.
        if not isinstance(key_node, yaml.ScalarNode) or key_node.style not in (None, ""):
            raise Refused(f"line {line}: a key that is no plain scalar")
        key = key_node.value
        if key == "<<":
            raise Refused(f"line {line}: the merge key <<")
        if key.casefold() in seen:
            raise Refused(f"line {line}: the key {key!r} repeats the key {seen[key.casefold()]!r}")
        seen[key.casefold()] = key
        mapping[key] = loader.construct_object(value_node, deep=True)
    return mapping


class WorkflowLoader(LOADER):
    """LOADER, with each mapping read by construct_mapping."""


WorkflowLoader.add_constructor("tag:yaml.org,2002:map", construct_mapping)


def workflow_files(root):
    """The names of the workflow files of the checkout `root`, sorted: GitHub runs each file of .github/workflows with
    one of these suffixes, and no file of a directory under it."""
    directory = pathlib.Path(root) / WORKFLOWS
    if not directory.is_dir():
        return []
    return sorted(path.name for path in directory.iterdir() if path.is_file() and path.suffix in (".yml", ".yaml"))


def text_of(root, path):
    """The text of the file `path`, from the top of the checkout `root`."""
    try:
        return (pathlib.Path(root) / path).read_text(encoding="utf-8")
    except (OSError, UnicodeDecodeError) as error:
        raise Unreadable(f"{path}: it cannot be read: {error}") from None


@functools.lru_cache(maxsize=None)
def parse(text, path):
    """The workflow `text` of the file `path`, parsed with WorkflowLoader: a mapping, else Unreadable. A file with an
    explicit tag, a merge key, a key that is no plain scalar or a key repeated in any case is Unreadable. Each text is
    parsed once in a process; no caller changes what it gets."""
    try:
        for token in yaml.scan(text, Loader=LOADER):
            if isinstance(token, yaml.TagToken):
                raise Refused(f"line {token.start_mark.line + 1}: an explicit tag")
        workflow = yaml.load(text, Loader=WorkflowLoader)
    except Refused as error:
        raise Unreadable(f"{path}: {error}, which GitHub and this check could read differently") from None
    except yaml.YAMLError as error:
        raise Unreadable(f"{path}: it is no YAML: {error}") from None
    if not isinstance(workflow, dict):
        raise Unreadable(f"{path}: it is no YAML mapping")
    return workflow


def load(root, path):
    """The workflow `path` of the checkout `root`, parsed."""
    return parse(text_of(root, path), path)


def jobs(workflow):
    """The jobs of a workflow, by id: each a mapping."""
    found = workflow.get("jobs")
    return {name: job for name, job in found.items() if isinstance(job, dict)} if isinstance(found, dict) else {}


def triggers(workflow):
    """The events of a workflow's on, as a dict."""
    on = workflow.get("on")
    if isinstance(on, str):
        return {on: None}
    if isinstance(on, list):
        return dict.fromkeys(on)
    return dict(on) if isinstance(on, dict) else {}


def condition(job):
    """A job's if, without ${{ }}, with each run of spaces as one space."""
    text = str(job.get("if", "")).strip()
    inner = re.fullmatch(r"\$\{\{(.*)\}\}", text, re.S)
    return " ".join((inner[1] if inner else text).split())


def expression_texts(text):
    """The text of each expression in a value, as GitHub reads it: from each ${{ to the first }} outside a string in
    single quotes (a quote in a string is written twice, which toggles twice), or to the end of the value when no }}
    ends it."""
    found, start = [], 0
    while (at := text.find(EXPRESSION_START, start)) >= 0:
        end, quoted = at + len(EXPRESSION_START), False
        while end < len(text) and (quoted or not text.startswith("}}", end)):
            quoted ^= text[end] == "'"
            end += 1
        found.append(text[at + len(EXPRESSION_START):end])
        start = end + 2
    return found


def expressions(value, key=None):
    """The text of each expression in a parsed YAML value: each ${{ }} of each key and each value, and each value of an
    if: whole, which GitHub reads as an expression with or without ${{ }}."""
    if isinstance(value, dict):
        return [text for name, item in value.items() for text in expressions(name) + expressions(item, name)]
    if isinstance(value, list):
        return [text for item in value for text in expressions(item)]
    if value is None:
        return []
    found = expression_texts(str(value))
    if key == "if":
        found.append(str(value))
    return found


def reads_secret(value):
    """Whether an expression in a parsed YAML value refers to the secrets context."""
    return any(SECRET.search(text) for text in expressions(value))


def held(workflow, job):
    """The credentials that `job` of `workflow` holds itself (the top of this file), each in words; none when it holds
    none. What a workflow that it calls holds is not counted here (holds)."""
    found = []
    if "environment" in job:
        environment = job["environment"]
        found.append(f"an environment ({environment.get('name') if isinstance(environment, dict) else environment})")
    if "secrets" in job:
        found.append("a secrets: key")
    if reads_secret([workflow.get("env"), job]):
        found.append("a read of a secret")
    permissions = job.get("permissions", workflow.get("permissions"))
    if permissions is None:
        if set(triggers(workflow)) - {"workflow_call"}:
            found.append("the token's default permissions (no permissions are given)")
    elif isinstance(permissions, dict):
        found += [f"{scope}: {level}" for scope, level in permissions.items() if level == "write"]
    elif permissions != "read-all":
        found.append(f"permissions {permissions}")
    uses = job.get("uses")
    if uses is not None and not LOCAL_WORKFLOW.fullmatch(str(uses)):
        found.append(f"a call of {uses}, a workflow of another repository, whose jobs this check cannot read")
    # main's Actions cache: review.yml restores entries of it for a review and trusts that only its session job saved
    # them. A run on pull_request saves only in the pull request's scope; a run on any other event can save in main's,
    # unless its cache-mode is none or read. A job that calls a workflow runs no step itself: its called jobs count.
    mode = job.get("cache-mode", workflow.get("cache-mode"))
    if uses is None and mode not in ("none", "read") and set(triggers(workflow)) - {"pull_request"}:
        found.append(f"write access to main's Actions cache (cache-mode: {mode or 'not given'})")
    return found


def holds(root, workflow, job, through=()):
    """What `job` of `workflow` holds (held), with what each job of the workflow of this repository that it calls
    holds, at every level, each in words. `through` holds the workflows on the way, which are not followed again."""
    found = held(workflow, job)
    called = LOCAL_WORKFLOW.fullmatch(str(job.get("uses", "")))
    if called and called[1] not in through and (pathlib.Path(root) / called[1]).is_file():
        inner = load(root, called[1])
        for name, inner_job in jobs(inner).items():
            found += [f"{what} in the job {name} of {called[1]}"
                      for what in holds(root, inner, inner_job, (*through, called[1]))]
    return found


def codeowner_problems(codeowners):
    """(c) for the text of CODEOWNERS, each as a problem: each line with no owner; each line of a pattern not in ALLOWED
    that names an owner; each line of a pattern of ALLOWED whose owners are not TEAMS alone; and each pattern of ALLOWED
    that no line names. A line whose first field starts with # is a comment, and a later field that starts with # ends
    the owners, as GitHub reads them."""
    problems, named = [], set()
    teams = " and ".join(TEAMS)
    for number, line in enumerate(codeowners.splitlines(), 1):
        fields = line.split()
        if not fields or fields[0].startswith("#"):
            continue
        pattern, rest = fields[0], fields[1:]
        given = rest[:next((i for i, field in enumerate(rest) if field.startswith("#")), len(rest))]
        where = f"{CODEOWNERS}, line {number}: {pattern}"
        named.add(pattern)
        if not given:
            problems.append(f"{where} names no owner: the last line that matches a file decides its owners, so a line "
                            "with no owner takes the owner back from each file of an earlier line that it matches")
        elif pattern not in ALLOWED:
            problems.append(f"{where} names {' '.join(given)}, but {CODEOWNERS} names an owner only for itself and "
                            "the agent instruction files (ALLOWED of .github/scripts/workflow-credentials): every "
                            "other file merges through the change review, the required check review")
        elif sorted(given) != sorted(TEAMS):
            problems.append(f"{where} names {' '.join(given)}, not {teams} alone")
    problems += [f"{CODEOWNERS}: no line names {pattern}, whose owners are {teams}: {CODEOWNERS} itself and the "
                 "agent instruction files keep their owner" for pattern in ALLOWED if pattern not in named]
    return problems


def pull_request_holds(root, name):
    """Each way the workflow .github/workflows/<name> of the checkout `root` breaks (r), as "<file>, <where>: <why>" or
    "<file>: <why>"; none when it starts neither on pull_request nor on pull_request_target. A job that calls a
    workflow of this repository (uses: ./.github/workflows/<file>) runs that file's jobs in its own run, with its own
    permissions at most: they are held to the same, and run only on push when the calling job does."""
    root = pathlib.Path(root)
    top = f"{WORKFLOWS}/{name}"
    problems = []

    def check(path, push_only, chain):
        text = text_of(root, path)
        workflow = parse(text, path)
        file = pathlib.PurePosixPath(path).name
        if reads_secret(workflow):
            problems.append(f"{file}: it reads a secret")
        holders = [("the workflow", workflow, push_only)] + [
            (f"the job {n}", job, push_only or condition(job) == PUSH_ONLY) for n, job in jobs(workflow).items()]
        for where, holder, push in holders:
            if holder is not workflow:
                if "environment" in holder:
                    problems.append(f"{file}, {where}: it has an environment")
                if "secrets" in holder:
                    problems.append(f"{file}, {where}: it passes secrets to {holder.get('uses')}")
                if "uses" in holder:
                    called = LOCAL_WORKFLOW.fullmatch(str(holder["uses"]))
                    if not called or not (root / called[1]).is_file():
                        problems.append(f"{file}, {where}: it calls {holder['uses']}, a workflow of no file here")
                    elif called[1] not in chain:
                        check(called[1], push, (*chain, called[1]))
            permissions = holder.get("permissions")
            if permissions is None:
                # A job with no permissions of its own takes its workflow's, which the workflow that starts on
                # pull_request must give. A called workflow's jobs take the calling job's.
                if holder is workflow and path == top:
                    problems.append(f"{file}, {where}: no permissions are given")
                continue
            if isinstance(permissions, str):
                # write-all grants id-token: write too, which even a push-only job must not hold.
                if permissions == "write-all":
                    problems.append(f"{file}, {where}: it has id-token: write")
                if permissions != "read-all":
                    problems.append(f"{file}, {where}: permissions {permissions}, but "
                                    + (PUSH_WRITES if push else f"its if is not {PUSH_ONLY}"))
                continue
            if not isinstance(permissions, dict):
                problems.append(f"{file}, {where}: permissions {permissions!r}, which GitHub does not read")
                continue
            if "id-token" in permissions:
                problems.append(f"{file}, {where}: it has id-token: {permissions['id-token']}")
            for scope, level in permissions.items():
                if level in ("read", "none"):
                    continue
                if holder is workflow:
                    why = "the workflow's permissions reach every job"
                elif not push:
                    why = f"its if is not {PUSH_ONLY}"
                elif (scope, level) != ("security-events", "write"):
                    why = PUSH_WRITES
                else:
                    continue
                problems.append(f"{file}, {where}: {scope}: {level}, but {why}")

    on = triggers(load(root, top))
    if "pull_request_target" in on:
        problems.append(f"{name}: it starts on pull_request_target")
    if "pull_request" in on:
        check(top, False, (top,))
    return problems


# The steps of github/codeql-action: init starts an analysis, analyze extracts and analyses. GitHub reads the owner and
# the repository of a uses: in any case, so these do too. An expression that names an input of the workflow.
CODEQL_INIT = re.compile(r"\s*github/codeql-action/init@", re.IGNORECASE)
CODEQL_STEP = re.compile(r"\s*github/codeql-action/", re.IGNORECASE)
INPUT = re.compile(r"\$\{\{\s*inputs\.([\w-]+)\s*\}\}")
# The languages whose CodeQL extractor reads source alone and runs none of it, in any build mode.
SOURCE_ONLY = ("actions", "javascript", "javascript-typescript", "typescript", "ruby")
# The compiled languages whose CodeQL extractor, in build mode none, reads source without a build and runs none of it:
# C and C++ alone. Another build mode (autobuild, manual) runs the project's build. C#'s extractor in build mode none
# runs dotnet restore on the repository's projects (github/codeql csharp/extractor/Semmle.Extraction.CSharp.
# DependencyFetching/NugetPackageRestorer.cs and DotNet.cs at e54be546741e764e42f1fe396b0a4816e28c327f), whose MSBuild
# targets can run code; Java's and Kotlin's read build files that can run code to resolve dependencies.
BUILDLESS = ("c", "cpp", "c-cpp")
# Python's extractor probes its interpreter with python -S -c "import gzip" in the checkout (github/codeql
# python/extractor/buildtools/index.py at the same commit, site_flag), and python -c puts the working directory first
# on sys.path: an entry at the top named for gzip, or for a module that gzip imports, would run. PYTHONSAFEPATH would
# keep it off sys.path, but then the extractor cannot import its own tracer. So Python reads source alone only in a job
# whose steps are: actions/checkout alone; then the step PYTHON_GUARD, its run exactly GUARD_RUN, which fails when an
# entry at the top of the checkout is named for a module of the standard library, with nothing that skips it, lets it
# fail or moves it (if, continue-on-error, working-directory, shell, a job's or a workflow's defaults); then CodeQL's
# steps alone, none with an if, so that nothing changes the top between the guard and the extraction and nothing runs
# CodeQL after a failed guard. Its CodeQL steps get no PYTHONPATH, which could name the checkout, and no step writes
# $GITHUB_ENV. Every other language counts as running code of the packages it reads: Rust's extractor loads each Cargo
# package with rust-analyzer, which runs cargo check, and with it each build script, and runs proc macros through the
# sysroot's proc-macro server (rust/extractor/src/config.rs at the same commit, lines 164 and 235:
# load_out_dirs_from_check: true), in any build mode; Go builds; and a language that this check does not know, or
# cannot read, counts too.
PYTHON_GUARD = "No entry at the top of the checkout is named for a module of Python's standard library"
GUARD_RUN = """python3 -I -c '
import os, sys
names = set(sys.stdlib_module_names)
found = sorted(entry for entry in os.listdir(".") if entry.split(".")[0] in names)
for entry in found:
    print("::error::" + entry + ": an entry at the top of the checkout named for the standard-library module "
          + entry.split(".")[0] + ", which CodeQL" + chr(39) + "s Python extractor could import from here")
sys.exit(1 if found else 0)
'
"""
CHECKOUT = re.compile(r"\s*actions/checkout@", re.IGNORECASE)
# How a problem says that python can import from the checkout.
PYTHON_UNSAFE = (" with the top of the checkout importable (no checkout, PYTHON_GUARD as written and CodeQL's steps "
                 "alone, in that order, a PYTHONPATH or a write of $GITHUB_ENV)")


def guards_python(workflow, job, steps):
    """Whether `steps` of `job` of `workflow` are actions/checkout alone, then PYTHON_GUARD as GUARD_RUN with no key
    but name and run, then github/codeql-action steps alone with no if, and neither the job nor the workflow sets
    defaults."""
    if workflow.get("defaults") is not None or job.get("defaults") is not None:
        return False
    at = next((index for index, step in enumerate(steps) if step.get("name") == PYTHON_GUARD), None)
    if at is None or set(steps[at]) != {"name", "run"} or steps[at]["run"] != GUARD_RUN:
        return False
    before, after = steps[:at], steps[at + 1:]
    return (all(CHECKOUT.match(str(step.get("uses", ""))) and "run" not in step for step in before)
            and bool(after) and all(CODEQL_STEP.match(str(step.get("uses", ""))) and "if" not in step
                                    and "run" not in step for step in after))


def runs_package_code(language, mode, safe_path):
    """Whether CodeQL's analysis of `language` in the build mode `mode` (None: not given, so CodeQL's default), in a
    job that runs PYTHON_GUARD before init and gives its CodeQL steps no PYTHONPATH when `safe_path`, can run code of
    the packages it reads."""
    if language in SOURCE_ONLY:
        return False
    if language == "python":
        return not safe_path
    return not (language in BUILDLESS and mode == "none")


def environment(workflow, job, step=None):
    """The env that `step` of `job` of `workflow` gets: the workflow's, then the job's, then the step's."""
    found = {}
    for env in (workflow.get("env"), job.get("env"), (step or {}).get("env")):
        if isinstance(env, dict):
            found.update({str(key): value for key, value in env.items()})
    return found


def analyses(root, workflow, job, given=None, through=()):
    """Each CodeQL analysis that `job` of `workflow` runs, at every level of calls, as (language, build mode,
    guard before init, where): from each step github/codeql-action/init, its languages and build-mode. A value
    ${{ inputs.<name> }} takes the input that the calling job gives (`given`, its with:, already resolved in the
    caller); any other expression, or a value that the check cannot resolve, is None. Languages are read as CodeQL
    reads them: lowercase, split at commas and blanks, empty ones dropped; none left, or none given, means that CodeQL
    finds them, so None. A call of a workflow that the check cannot read (of another repository, or of no file here)
    is one analysis of None: the check must not pass what it could not read."""
    def resolve(value):
        if value is None:
            return None
        match = INPUT.fullmatch(str(value).strip())
        if match:
            value = None if given is None else given.get(match[1])
            if value is None:
                return None
        return None if "${{" in str(value) else str(value)

    found = []
    steps = [step for step in job.get("steps") or [] if isinstance(step, dict)]
    codeql = [environment(workflow, job, step) for step in steps if CODEQL_STEP.match(str(step.get("uses", "")))]
    safe_path = (guards_python(workflow, job, steps) and all(env.get("PYTHONPATH") is None for env in codeql)
                 and not any("GITHUB_ENV" in str(step.get("run", "")) for step in steps))
    for step in job.get("steps") or []:
        if isinstance(step, dict) and CODEQL_INIT.match(str(step.get("uses", ""))):
            inputs = step.get("with") or {}
            languages = resolve(inputs.get("languages"))
            mode = resolve(inputs.get("build-mode"))
            mode = mode.strip().lower() if mode else None
            names = [part for part in re.split(r"[\s,]+", languages.lower()) if part] if languages else []
            found += [(language, mode, safe_path, "") for language in names or [None]]
    uses = job.get("uses")
    if uses is None:
        return found
    called = LOCAL_WORKFLOW.fullmatch(str(uses))
    if not called or not (pathlib.Path(root) / called[1]).is_file():
        return found + [(None, None, False, f" in {uses}, which this check cannot read")]
    if called[1] in through:
        return found
    inner = load(root, called[1])
    passed = {str(key): resolve(value) for key, value in (job.get("with") or {}).items()}
    for name, inner_job in jobs(inner).items():
        found += [(language, mode, safe, f" in the job {name} of {called[1]}{where}")
                  for language, mode, safe, where in analyses(root, inner, inner_job, passed, (*through, called[1]))]
    return found


def package_code_problems(root, names):
    """Each job of the workflows `names` that holds a credential (holds) and runs a CodeQL analysis that can run code of
    the packages it reads (runs_package_code), as a problem: that code comes from files that need no code owner, and it
    would run with the credential."""
    problems = []
    for name in names:
        path = f"{WORKFLOWS}/{name}"
        workflow = load(root, path)
        if set(triggers(workflow)) == {"workflow_call"}:
            # Its jobs run only when a job calls it: they are read there, with the inputs that job gives.
            continue
        for job_name, job in jobs(workflow).items():
            what = ", ".join(holds(root, workflow, job, (path,)))
            if not what:
                continue
            for language, mode, safe_path, where in analyses(root, workflow, job, through=(path,)):
                if runs_package_code(language, mode, safe_path):
                    without = PYTHON_UNSAFE if language == "python" else ""
                    problems.append(
                        f"{path}: the job {job_name} holds {what} and runs CodeQL's analysis of "
                        f"{language or 'languages this check cannot read'} in build mode {mode or 'not given'}"
                        f"{without}{where}, which can run code of the packages it reads (build scripts, proc macros, "
                        "a build or the checkout's modules), from files that need no code owner")
    return problems


def credentials(workflow, job, inherited=None):
    """What `job` of `workflow` can get a credential with, for (m), each in words; none when nothing. Its permissions
    are its own, else its workflow's, else `inherited`, the calling job's when the workflow is called. With none at all
    the token has the repository's default permissions, which give no identity token."""
    found = []
    permissions = job.get("permissions", workflow.get("permissions", inherited))
    if permissions == "write-all" or (isinstance(permissions, dict) and any(
            str(scope).casefold() == "id-token" and str(level).casefold() == "write"
            for scope, level in permissions.items())):
        found.append("id-token: write")
    if reads_secret([workflow.get("env"), job]):
        found.append("a read of a secret")
    return found


def environment_name(job):
    """The name of the environment that `job` declares, as written, or None."""
    environment = job.get("environment")
    return environment.get("name") if isinstance(environment, dict) else environment


def main_only(root, names):
    """(m) for the workflows `names` of the checkout `root`: (each problem, each job that can get a credential as (its
    workflow's path, its name, what it can get one with, the environment it declares or None)). A workflow that starts
    only on workflow_call is read through the jobs that call it, with their events and permissions."""
    root = pathlib.Path(root)
    problems, found = [], []
    every = ", ".join(MAIN_ONLY_ENVIRONMENTS)

    def check(path, workflow, on, name, job, inherited, chain, via):
        where = f"{path}: the job {name}{via}"
        uses = job.get("uses")
        if uses is not None:
            permissions = job.get("permissions", workflow.get("permissions", inherited))
            if "secrets" in job:
                problems.append(f"{where} passes secrets to {uses}, but a job that calls a workflow declares no "
                                "environment: the called job that needs a secret declares its own")
            called = LOCAL_WORKFLOW.fullmatch(str(uses))
            if not called or not (root / called[1]).is_file():
                if credentials({}, {}, permissions):
                    problems.append(f"{where} calls {uses}, whose jobs this check cannot read, with id-token: write")
                return
            if called[1] not in chain:
                inner = load(root, called[1])
                for inner_name, inner_job in jobs(inner).items():
                    check(called[1], inner, on, inner_name, inner_job, permissions, (*chain, called[1]),
                          f", which the job {name} of {path} calls,")
            return
        what = credentials(workflow, job, inherited)
        if not what:
            return
        holds = " and ".join(what)
        named = environment_name(job)
        found.append((path, name, holds, named))
        if named is None:
            problems.append(f"{where} can get a credential ({holds}) and declares no environment, so a run of any "
                            f"branch gets it: it must declare one of {every}, whose one deployment branch is main")
        elif str(named) not in MAIN_ONLY_ENVIRONMENTS:
            problems.append(f"{where} can get a credential ({holds}) in the environment {named}, which is not one "
                            f"of the main-only environments {every}")
        for event in PULL_REQUEST_EVENTS:
            if event in on:
                problems.append(f"{where} can get a credential ({holds}) and its workflow starts on {event}, where it "
                                "would run beside a pull request's code")
        for step in job.get("steps") or []:
            if not isinstance(step, dict):
                continue
            given = step.get("with") if isinstance(step.get("with"), dict) else {}
            other = [f"{key} {value}" for key, value in given.items() if str(key).casefold() in ("repository", "ref")]
            if CHECKOUT.match(str(step.get("uses", ""))) and other:
                problems.append(f"{where} can get a credential ({holds}) and checks out {', '.join(other)}: a job "
                                "that can get one checks out only its run's own commit")
            if PULL_REQUEST_CODE.search(str(step.get("run", ""))):
                problems.append(f"{where} can get a credential ({holds}) and a step runs gh pr checkout or git "
                                "fetch, checkout, switch, worktree, pull or reset, or names refs/pull/, which can put "
                                "other code than its run's own commit in its workspace")

    for file in names:
        path = f"{WORKFLOWS}/{file}"
        workflow = load(root, path)
        on = triggers(workflow)
        if set(on) == {"workflow_call"}:
            continue
        for name, job in jobs(workflow).items():
            check(path, workflow, on, name, job, None, (path,), "")
    return problems, found


def main(argv):
    root = pathlib.Path(argv[1] if len(argv) > 1 else ".")
    names = workflow_files(root)
    if not names:
        print(f"::error::{root / WORKFLOWS} holds no workflow file (*.yml, *.yaml): nothing was checked.")
        return 1
    try:
        problems = codeowner_problems(text_of(root, CODEOWNERS))
        starts = 0
        for name in names:
            starts += "pull_request" in triggers(load(root, f"{WORKFLOWS}/{name}"))
            problems += pull_request_holds(root, name)
        problems += package_code_problems(root, names)
        main_problems, credentialed = main_only(root, names)
        problems += main_problems
    except Unreadable as error:
        print(f"::error::{error}")
        return 1
    for problem in problems:
        print(f"::error::{problem}")
    if problems:
        return 1
    print(f"Read {len(names)} workflow files and {CODEOWNERS}, which gives the {len(ALLOWED)} patterns of the agent "
          f"instruction files and itself to {' and '.join(TEAMS)}, and no other file to anyone (c). Starting on "
          f"pull_request: {starts}, and none breaks (r). No job with a credential runs a CodeQL analysis that can run "
          "code of the packages it reads. Jobs that can get a credential, each in a main-only environment (m): "
          f"{len(credentialed)}"
          + (f" ({'; '.join(f'{path} {name} in {named}' for path, name, _, named in credentialed)})."
             if credentialed else ", so (m) had no job to check."))
    return 0


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main(sys.argv))
